WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:04.980
MODOK is finally in the MCU,&nbsp;
so why are some Marvel fans so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:08.400
grumpy about it? It's hard out there&nbsp;
when you're just a head in a chair.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.440
Audiences around the world will soon get an&nbsp;
up-close and personal look at the Quantum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:18.540
Realm in "Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania". As&nbsp;
the new trailer dropped on January 10th reveals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:23.640
Scott Lang is whisked once again into this&nbsp;
bizarre and fascinating pocket dimension,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.600
where he's greeted by alien landscapes&nbsp;
and some imposing new characters.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:32.160
The major villain of "Quantumania" is&nbsp;
Kang the Conqueror, but there's another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.120
famous Marvel villain lurking in the&nbsp;
background in the new trailer — MODOK,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.333
who has long been speculated&nbsp;
to have a role in the movie.

00:00:39.333 --> 00:00:43.233
"I'm a big deal! I just fought&nbsp;
Iron Man! I stole his boot!"

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.180
Comic book aficionados or fans of the&nbsp;
Hulu show know that MODOK stands for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:54.360
"Mental Organism Designed Only for Killing,"&nbsp;
and he has a rather distinct look. MODOK is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.580
essentially a giant head wrapped in advanced&nbsp;
robotics with tiny, almost vestigial, limbs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:04.800
By no means does that make MODOK a pushover,&nbsp;
and although his physical form is peculiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.300
his mental prowess is more than capable of&nbsp;
giving superheroes a run for their money.

00:01:09.300 --> 00:01:14.580
MODOK was originally a human technician named&nbsp;
George Tarleton, who was transformed by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:19.080
villainous organization known as A.I.M.,&nbsp;
or Advanced Idea Mechanics. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.560
it seems as if "Ant-Man and the Wasp:&nbsp;
Quantumania" is taking a slightly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:27.240
different approach to the source material, and&nbsp;
bringing back Ant-Man's original movie foe.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:28.980
A.I.M. has already been brought up in the Marvel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.980 --> 00:01:33.120
Cinematic Universe in "Iron Man 3" as&nbsp;
the think-tank for Aldrich Killian.

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:37.140
"Advanced Idea Mechanics, or&nbsp;
AIM, for short. You get it?"

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.640
"I see that, 'cuz it's on your t-shirt!"

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.840
"Ah!"

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:44.640
Plus, Tarleton hasn't shown up, so it seems&nbsp;
as if MODOK will instead be Darren Cross,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.100
or at least some variation of the character.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:52.320
When audiences last saw Cross, he was battling&nbsp;
Ant-Man at the end of the first "Ant-Man" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.000
but Cross was eventually defeated and shrunk&nbsp;
down to nothingness — or at least it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.200
assumed. Now that the second trailer for&nbsp;
"Ant-Man and the Wasp: Quantumania" is out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.980
it seems as if fans finally know what&nbsp;
happened to Cross after that battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:09.000
as he is spotted hovering behind&nbsp;
Kang as the iconic villain.

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:12.780
When it comes to the nature and look&nbsp;
of MODOK, it looks like fans are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:17.160
already fiercely divided in opinions of the&nbsp;
big-screen version of this famous villain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:22.140
and have converged on Twitter to let loose&nbsp;
a mighty deluge of thoughts and comments.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.080
On the negative side of&nbsp;
opinions, one viewer wrote,

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.780
Another fan had some strong words, posting,

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:45.420
This is a reference to Lopez's character Mr.&nbsp;
Electric from "The Adventures Of Sharkboy And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:50.520
Lavagirl," who also sports a bulbous head,&nbsp;
and was noted several times in fan comments.

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:54.840
On the flip side, there are some fans&nbsp;
who absolutely love the MODOK in "Ant-Man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.300
and the Wasp: Quantumania,"&nbsp;
so what are their thoughts?

